"Sourcing the ``local_setup`` of the overlay will only add the packages "
"available in the overlay to your environment. ``setup`` sources the overlay "
"as well as the underlay it was created in, allowing you to utilize both "
"workspaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:363
msgid ""
"So, sourcing your main ROS 2 installation's ``setup`` and then the "
"``ros2_ws`` overlay's ``local_setup``, like you just did, is the same as "
"just sourcing ``ros2_ws``'s ``setup``, because that includes the environment "
"of its underlay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:366
msgid "Now you can run the ``turtlesim`` package from the overlay:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:368
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:393
#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:406
msgid "$ ros2 run turtlesim turtlesim_node"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:372
msgid ""
"But how can you tell that this is the overlay turtlesim running, and not "
"your main installation's turtlesim?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:374
msgid "Let's modify turtlesim in the overlay so you can see the effects:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:376
msgid ""
"You can modify and rebuild packages in the overlay separately from the "
"underlay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:377
msgid "The overlay takes precedence over the underlay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:381
msgid "7 Modify the overlay"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:383
msgid ""
"You can modify ``turtlesim`` in your overlay by editing the title bar on the "
"turtlesim window. To do this, locate the ``turtle_frame.cpp`` file in ``~/"
"ros2_ws/src/ros_tutorials/turtlesim/src``. Open ``turtle_frame.cpp`` with "
"your preferred text editor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:387
msgid ""
"Find the function ``setWindowTitle(\"TurtleSim\");``, change the value "
"``\"TurtleSim\"`` to ``\"MyTurtleSim\"``, and save the file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:389
msgid ""
"Return to the first terminal where you ran ``colcon build`` earlier and run "
"it again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:391
msgid ""
"Return to the second terminal (where the overlay is sourced) and run "
"turtlesim again:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:397
msgid ""
"You will see the title bar on the turtlesim window now says \"MyTurtleSim\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:401
msgid ""
"Even though your main ROS 2 environment was sourced in this terminal "
"earlier, the overlay of your ``ros2_ws`` environment takes precedence over "
"the contents of the underlay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:403
msgid ""
"To see that your underlay is still intact, open a brand new terminal and "
"source only your ROS 2 installation. Run turtlesim again:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:412
msgid ""
"You can see that modifications in the overlay did not actually affect "
"anything in the underlay."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:416
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:417
msgid ""
"In this tutorial, you sourced your main ROS 2 distro install as your "
"underlay, and created an overlay by cloning and building packages in a new "
"workspace. The overlay gets prepended to the path, and takes precedence over "
"the underlay, as you saw with your modified turtlesim."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/ROS-Framework/client-libraries/Working-with-Client-Libraries/Creating-A-Workspace/Creating-A-Workspace.rst:420
msgid ""
"Using overlays is recommended for working on a small number of packages, so "
"you don't have to put everything in the same workspace and rebuild a huge "
"workspace on every iteration."
msgstr ""

